-- title:
--   Theorem 2, p. 216 — the limit SDE $dq = f(q)\,dt + \sqrt{2\mu}\,db$ has a unique strong solution
-- statement:
--   Let $\mu > 0$, $\theta > 0$ and $\beta \in \mathbb R$, and let
--   $$
--   f(x) = \begin{cases} -\mu(\beta + x), & x \le 0,\\ -(\mu\beta + \theta x), & x > 0.\end{cases}
--   $$
--   Let $(\Omega, \mathcal F, P)$ be a probability space carrying a standard Brownian motion $b$ (every path continuous) and a real random variable $X_0$ independent of $b$. Then:
--
--   1. **Existence.** There is a process $q$ with $q(0) = X_0$, continuous paths on $[0,\infty)$, each $q(t)$ measurable with respect to $\sigma(X_0, b(s) : s \le t)$, such that almost surely, for all $t \ge 0$,
--   $$
--   q(t) = q(0) + \int_0^t f(q(s))\,ds + \sqrt{2\mu}\, b(t).
--   $$
--   2. **Pathwise uniqueness.** If $q$ and $q'$ both solve this equation driven by the same $b$ and $q(0) = q'(0)$ almost surely, then almost surely $q(t) = q'(t)$ for all $t \ge 0$.
--
--   This makes "$q$ is the unique solution of the stochastic differential equation" in Theorem 2 meaningful; the paper omits the discussion and refers to Dupuis and Ishii (1993) and Mandelbaum and Pats (1995).
--
--   **Formalization Note** Because the noise is additive and $f$ is Lipschitz, the equation is written pathwise with a Lebesgue integral; a solution is a strong solution. The hypotheses $\theta > 0$, $\mu > 0$ are those of Theorem 2.
-- source:
--   Garnett, Mandelbaum & Reiman, Designing a Call Center with Impatient Customers, M&SOM 4(3), 2002, p. 216, Theorem 2 ("q is the unique solution"); p. 224, App. C (uniqueness remark)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ErlangA_Diffusion_SDE

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Filter Topology
open scoped ENNReal NNReal

namespace ErlangA.Diffusion

/-- Well-posedness of the limit SDE of Theorem 2 (p. 216; p. 224). Let `μ > 0`, `θ > 0` and
`β ∈ ℝ`. On a probability space carrying a standard Brownian motion `b` (every path continuous)
and a real random variable `X₀` independent of `b`:
1. (existence) there is a process `q` with `q(0) = X₀`, adapted to the filtration generated by
   `X₀` and `b`, solving `dq(t) = f(q) dt + √(2μ) db(t)`;
2. (pathwise uniqueness) any two solutions driven by the same `b` with almost surely equal
   initial values are indistinguishable on `[0, ∞)`. -/
theorem sde_well_posed (μ β θ : ℝ) (hμ : 0 < μ) (hθ : 0 < θ)
    {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure P]
    (b : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) (hb : IsStandardBM P b)
    (X0 : Ω → ℝ) (hX0 : Measurable X0)
    (hind : IndepFun X0 (fun ω (t : ℝ≥0) => b t ω) P) :
    (∃ q : Ω → ℝ → ℝ, (∀ ω, q ω 0 = X0 ω) ∧ IsAdaptedToInitialAndBM b X0 q ∧
      SolvesDiffusionSDE P μ β θ b q) ∧
    ∀ q q' : Ω → ℝ → ℝ, SolvesDiffusionSDE P μ β θ b q → SolvesDiffusionSDE P μ β θ b q' →
      (∀ᵐ ω ∂P, q ω 0 = q' ω 0) → ∀ᵐ ω ∂P, ∀ t : ℝ, 0 ≤ t → q ω t = q' ω t := by sorry

end ErlangA.Diffusion
